%N A400708 Number of adjacent and contiguous buildings that can be seen from a flagpole of height n placed just left of the City Buildings shown in sequence A400000 (see Comments for precise definition).
%C A400708 On the number line, erect a flagpole of height n at 0. At each position k >= 1, erect a building of height b(k)+1, where b(k) is the City Buildings sequence A400000. That is, the buildings are as in A400000 but raised up by one floor.
%C A400708 An observer sits at the top of the pole, and looks down. He is not allowed to look at buildings at his own height, he can only look downwards. He counts how many consecutive buildings he can see, starting with the buildings of heights 1, 2, 2, 3, 2, 3, 2, 4, ...
%C A400708 He stops counting at the point when a building is hidden from him. The number of contiguous buildings that he can see is a(n). For n = 0, 1, 2, 3, ..., the number that he can see is 0, 0, 1, 3, 4, 4, 4, 6, 6, 8, ...
%C A400708 The conventions about buildings on the same line of sight being visible are as in A400000.
%C A400708 For the runs of consecutive equal terms, see A400709.
%H A400708 N. J. A. Sloane, <a href="/A400708/b400708.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..1000</a>
